conmon: accept the console and attach connections when the runtime exits before the loop sees them

For `exec -t`, the runtime connects to the console socket and sends the
terminal before it exits. conmon accepts that connection from a main-loop
callback, and quits the loop from runtime_exit_cb when it reaps the runtime.
The two can be dispatched from different polls: a callback that reaps
children (the initial child check, or a SIGCHLD from the exec'd process,
which is reparented to conmon and can exit first) runs waitpid() after the
poll that scheduled it. When the runtime connects and exits in between,
runtime_exit_cb quits the loop with the connection still queued, and conmon
exits with "Runtime did not set up terminal" without writing to the sync
pipe. podman then reports `container create failed (no logs from conmon):
conmon bytes ""` for an exec whose process already ran.

After the loop, if the runtime succeeded and no terminal was received, take
a connection that is already queued on the console socket.

The attach connection can be queued for the same reason. podman connects to
the attach socket before it lets the runtime start, so when the poll before
the reaping callback ran ahead of that connect, the loop quits with it still
queued. If the exec'd process has also exited, conmon does not run its
second loop: it drains the terminal with no attach connection accepted,
which drops the output, and exits, which resets the queued connection.
podman reads the reset as end of output, so the exec exits 0 with no
output. So after taking the late console, also accept the attach
connections already queued.

Carried by fcvm (issue #841). The race is still present in upstream conmon
main, which since 05eff515 reports the same failure on the sync pipe.

--- a/src/conmon.c
+++ b/src/conmon.c
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 #include <locale.h>
+#include <poll.h>
 
